<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 2: Demystifying AI                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--
  PLAN (agreed with Lily):
  - Hook / case study: the Kenyan workers (employed by Sama, in Nairobi) who
    labelled harmful text so that ChatGPT could be made safer. Research notes
    with sources and the disputed points are in notes/ch2-sama-openai-research.md
  - Then explain how AI learns, using the postcode-sorting example from the
    podcast (humans in the loop, feedback), the birdsong/toad example (limits),
    and a short mention that the boundary of what AI can do keeps moving.
  - Chapter 3 (Illusion of Autonomy) gets the Amazon "Just Walk Out" story and
    the Meta moderators' court cases as its hidden-labour examples.
  - The Dutch childcare scandal belongs in the Misuse and Misinterpretation
    chapter.
-->
<chapter xml:id="ch-demystifying-ai" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Demystifying AI</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-demystifying-ai">
        <p>
            Ask ChatGPT for something truly vile and it'll politely refuse.
            That didn't happen by accident, and it isn't magic.
            Somebody had to teach it what <q>vile</q> looks like.
            According to a TIME investigation, some of those people were in
            Nairobi, and they were paid less than $2 an hour to do it.
        </p>

        <p>
            That's where this chapter starts.
            It's about what's really going on inside AI.
            A lot of what's said about AI makes it sound like something that
            thinks for itself, and a lot of what's said against it makes it
            sound like something to fear.
            Neither helps much.
            If you understand how these systems are made, and how they learn,
            you can use them more sensibly, and ask better questions about
            them.
        </p>

        <note>
            <title>Content warning</title>
            <p>
                The case study mentions sexual abuse, violence and self-harm,
                in general terms.
                We don't describe any of the content itself.
            </p>
        </note>

        <!-- Draft objectives: revise once the rest of the chapter is written. -->
        <objectives>
            <title>By the end of this chapter you should be able to</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain how a system like ChatGPT is made safer, and
                        the part that people play in it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe how a machine learning system improves when
                        humans give it feedback.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain why an AI can sound confident and still be
                        wrong.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Say why nobody can say for sure where the line between
                        what AI can and can't do will end up.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 1. Case study                                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-demystifying-ai-case-study">
        <title>Case Study: The Workers Behind the Safety Filter</title>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-problem">
            <title>The problem: a chatbot that said terrible things</title>

            <p>
                ChatGPT's predecessor, GPT-3, was trained on hundreds of
                billions of words taken from the internet.
                It could write fluent sentences, but it was also prone to
                producing violent, sexist and racist remarks.
                That's not surprising, because the internet is full of all of
                those, and there was no easy way to take the toxic parts out
                of what it had learned from
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                OpenAI's answer was to build another AI whose only job was to
                spot toxic text.
                It could be built into ChatGPT to filter out harmful answers
                before you saw them, and it could also be used to clean up the
                data that future models learned from
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                But how do you teach a machine to recognise violence or hate
                speech?
                You show it lots of examples that have already been labelled by
                a person.
                This example is violence, this one is hate speech, and so on.
                That's the step that needs people.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-demystifying-ai-labelling">
                <title>Data labelling</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <term>Data labelling</term>, sometimes called
                        <term>annotation</term>, is when people mark examples
                        with the right answer, so that a machine learning
                        system has something to learn from.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-work">
            <title>The work</title>

            <p>
                From November 2021, OpenAI sent tens of thousands of snippets
                of text to a company called Sama.
                Sama is based in San Francisco, has workers in Kenya, Uganda
                and India, and describes itself as an <q>ethical AI</q>
                company.
                In Nairobi, about three dozen workers were split into three
                teams, one each for sexual abuse, hate speech and violence.
                Their job was to read the text and label it
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Much of the text was graphic.
                TIME reports that it included descriptions of child sexual
                abuse, murder, suicide, torture and self-harm.
                TIME spoke to four of the workers, who stayed anonymous because
                they were worried about their jobs.
                One described having recurring visions after reading a
                particular passage, and called the work <q>torture</q>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Some of the numbers are disputed.
                <xref ref="tbl-demystifying-ai-disputed"/> puts TIME's reporting
                next to what Sama said in reply.
                We've converted the dollar amounts at about KES 130 to the
                dollar, which is the 2026 rate, not the 2022 one.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tbl-demystifying-ai-disputed">
                <title>What TIME reported, and what Sama said</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>TIME's reporting</cell>
                        <cell>Sama's reply</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Pay per hour, after tax</cell>
                        <cell>About $1.32 to $2 (about KES 170 to KES 260),
                        depending on seniority and performance</cell>
                        <cell>$1.46 to $3.74 (about KES 190 to KES 490)</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Passages to read per nine-hour shift</cell>
                        <cell>150 to 250, according to three workers</cell>
                        <cell>70, and <q>not up to 250</q></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>Counselling</cell>
                        <cell>All four workers said the <q>wellness</q>
                        sessions were rare and unhelpful</cell>
                        <cell>Individual and group sessions with licensed
                        therapists were available at any time</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                Even the number of people involved varies.
                TIME says about three dozen.
                The workers who petitioned Parliament, and the Guardian, say
                51, and the Wall Street Journal says about 50.
                And one of the workers, Mophat, told the Guardian that he read
                up to 700 passages a day, which is far more than either figure
                in the table
                (<xref ref="biblio-guardian-2023" text="custom">The Guardian, 2023</xref>;
                <xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Some things aren't in dispute.
                The contracts said that OpenAI would pay Sama $12.50 an hour
                (about KES 1,625) for the work.
                Sama says that covers all its costs, including salaries,
                benefits and infrastructure.
                TIME says that was six to nine times what the workers took
                home.
                For the most junior labellers, the basic salary was KES 21,000
                a month, plus a bonus of about $70 (about KES 9,100) for
                working with explicit content.
                For comparison, TIME gives the minimum wage for a receptionist
                in Nairobi at the time as $1.52 an hour
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                OpenAI's position is that it didn't set productivity targets,
                and that Sama was responsible for pay and for mental health
                support.
                It also told the Wall Street Journal that it wasn't aware the
                workers were getting only a small fraction of the $12.50.
                Sama told the Journal that its leadership didn't know about the
                psychological impact of the project, and that as well as
                counselling it offered prayer and meditation rooms
                (<xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
                It also says that labelling harmful text and images is
                <q>a necessary step</q> in making its tools safer
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-accounts">
            <title>Their accounts</title>

            <p>
                So far we've mostly had numbers.
                Here are some of the workers' own accounts, as they told them to
                journalists at the Guardian and the Wall Street Journal.
                We've put them in our own words.
                They're the workers' accounts of what happened, and as you've
                seen, Sama disputes parts of them.
            </p>

            <assemblage xml:id="asm-demystifying-ai-mophat">
                <title>Mophat Okinyi</title>
                <p>
                    Mophat was on the team reading sexual content.
                    He told the Guardian that he read up to 700 passages a day.
                    He started avoiding people, and found himself imagining the
                    worst about the people around him.
                    His wife, who was pregnant at the time, told him he was a
                    changed man, and left.
                    He told the Guardian that he had lost his family.
                </p>
                <p>
                    He told the Wall Street Journal that he found he didn't want
                    to be close to his stepdaughter, whom he thinks of as his
                    daughter, because of what he'd read.
                    He tried the counselling Sama offered, but said the
                    sessions asked him basic questions, like how his day had
                    been, after he'd spent eight hours reading those texts.
                </p>
                <p>
                    He says he's proud to have helped make ChatGPT safe, but he
                    asks himself whether what he put in was worth what he got
                    back.
                    He told the Guardian that he thinks of the ChatGPT users he
                    protected, and sees himself like a soldier who takes bullets
                    for other people
                    (<xref ref="biblio-guardian-2023" text="custom">The Guardian, 2023</xref>;
                    <xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <assemblage xml:id="asm-demystifying-ai-alex">
                <title>Alex Kairu</title>
                <p>
                    Alex was hired by Sama in May 2021.
                    He told the Wall Street Journal that he'd been promised
                    skills, training and a career.
                    He ended up on the team reading violent content.
                </p>
                <p>
                    He had nightmares, and became afraid of people.
                    He asked his brother to sit with him until he fell asleep,
                    because he was afraid of what he'd do if he slept alone.
                    He told the Guardian that before the project he was
                    outgoing and loved DJing, at churches and parties.
                    Afterwards he became introverted, his relationship with his
                    wife suffered, and he moved back in with his parents.
                </p>
                <p>
                    When the OpenAI contract ended, Sama offered him a new role,
                    labelling images of cars, but his mental health was getting
                    worse.
                    He wishes that someone had asked him what he was going
                    through.
                    In July 2023, he was unemployed
                    (<xref ref="biblio-guardian-2023" text="custom">The Guardian, 2023</xref>;
                    <xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <assemblage xml:id="asm-demystifying-ai-richard">
                <title>Richard Mathenge</title>
                <p>
                    Richard told the Guardian that for him and his colleagues,
                    this was a first job, and they were desperate for work.
                    The week of training was friendly, and the content seemed
                    average.
                    As the project went on, the passages got longer and more
                    disturbing.
                </p>
                <p>
                    His team ended each day on a group call, swapping stories of
                    the worst things they'd read, a way of coping.
                    They worked in a separate part of the office, so no one
                    could see what was on their screens.
                    He says he contacted Sama's wellness department about what
                    the team was going through, and found them non-committal.
                </p>
                <p>
                    When the contract ended eight months early, he says they
                    were left without an income while dealing with serious
                    trauma
                    (<xref ref="biblio-guardian-2023" text="custom">The Guardian, 2023</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <assemblage xml:id="asm-demystifying-ai-bill">
                <title>Bill Mulinya</title>
                <p>
                    Bill saw the team leader job advertised on LinkedIn.
                    He told the Wall Street Journal that his team began by
                    labelling images, like traffic signs, cars, roads and trees.
                    In November 2021 the work changed to reading and labelling
                    text, and at first they weren't even told that the client
                    was OpenAI.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Afterwards, he and Mophat began organising.
                    They had met people at other companies doing content
                    moderation, and found that their experiences were much the
                    same.
                    Rather than each person fighting alone, they decided to form
                    a union, which by July 2023 had over 150 members from
                    several companies
                    (<xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
                </p>
            </assemblage>

            <p>
                You can also watch Mophat tell his story in a short BBC News
                video
                (<xref ref="biblio-bbc-video" text="custom">BBC News, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about three and a half minutes.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-ending">
            <title>How it ended</title>

            <p>
                In February 2022, Sama began a pilot for a different OpenAI
                project, collecting sexual and violent images, some of them
                illegal.
                Sama sent a sample of 1,400 images, and OpenAI paid $787.50
                (about KES 102,000) for them.
                OpenAI says it never meant to collect the most serious category,
                child sexual abuse material.
                Sama says that when it saw the instructions, its team raised
                concerns, and it ended the pilot and cancelled the rest of its
                OpenAI work
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Sama's last batch of labelled text went to OpenAI in March
                2022, eight months before the contract was due to finish.
                Sama's reason, as it told TIME, is that it didn't want to
                expose its staff to that content again.
                It has given other explanations too.
                It told the Guardian it was to focus on computer vision data
                annotation, and it told the Wall Street Journal it followed a
                dispute with OpenAI over a related project
                (<xref ref="biblio-guardian-2023" text="custom">The Guardian, 2023</xref>;
                <xref ref="biblio-wsj-2023" text="custom">Wall Street Journal, 2023</xref>).
                Some workers say that managers gave them a different reason,
                which was a TIME story, published in February 2022, about
                Facebook moderators at Sama.
                Most of the workers were moved to lower-paid work, without the
                bonus, and some lost their jobs.
                In January 2023, Sama announced it was leaving content
                moderation altogether, including a $3.9 million Facebook
                contract (about KES 507 million), which cost around 200 jobs
                in Nairobi
                (<xref ref="biblio-time-2023" text="custom">Perrigo, 2023</xref>).
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-after">
            <title>What happened next</title>

            <p>
                In July 2023, workers who had helped remove harmful content from
                ChatGPT petitioned Kenya's Parliament.
                They asked it to investigate the work and operations of Big
                Tech firms that outsource through companies like Sama, and to
                bring in laws to regulate outsourced, dangerous technology work
                (<xref ref="biblio-techcrunch-2023" text="custom">Njanja, 2023b</xref>).
                The workers say they had no psychosocial support, and that they
                developed PTSD, paranoia, depression, anxiety and insomnia.
                Those are the workers' claims, and Sama disputes parts of them.
                UK non-profit Foxglove backed the petition
                (<xref ref="biblio-itweb-2023" text="custom">Mungadze, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                Sama said it was open to working with the Kenyan government and
                welcomed third-party audits.
                OpenAI said the work was challenging, and that it had shared
                ethical and wellness standards with the people doing it.
                ITWeb reported that OpenAI didn't respond to the specific
                mental health claims
                (<xref ref="biblio-itweb-2023" text="custom">Mungadze, 2023</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                The story hasn't stopped.
                In 2026, 404 Media reported on a Data Labelers Association in
                Kenya, which is signing up workers and asking for better pay,
                better mental health services, and an end to what it calls
                draconian non-disclosure agreements
                (<xref ref="biblio-404media-2026" text="custom">Koebler, 2026</xref>).
            </p>

            <p>
                There have also been court cases in Kenya, but they're about a
                different group.
                In 2023, 183 former Facebook content moderators, who had been
                employed by Sama, sued Meta, Sama and another contractor, and
                a Kenyan court ruled that Meta can be sued in Kenya
                (<xref ref="biblio-techcrunch-court-2023" text="custom">Njanja, 2023a</xref>).
                We couldn't find a court case brought by the ChatGPT labellers
                themselves.
                The moderators' case comes up again in the next chapter.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-lessons">
            <title>What the case shows</title>

            <p>
                When you use a chatbot, it's easy to think of it as a machine
                that just knows how to behave.
                This story says otherwise.
                The safety filter works because people read huge amounts of
                harmful text first, and told the system what it was looking at.
                That isn't a flaw in the way AI is built.
                It's how this kind of AI learns.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    When you use an AI tool, ask who taught it, and who paid
                    for that.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                It also gives the idea of a <q>human in the loop</q> from the
                last chapter a face.
                The human in the loop isn't an abstract safeguard.
                Here, it was a few dozen people in Nairobi reading things most
                of us would never want to read, for pay that two companies
                disagree about.
                If AI is going to be used responsibly, how those people are
                paid and looked after has to be part of the question, along with
                what the model does.
            </p>

            <p>
                So why does a machine need people at all?
                To answer that, we need to look at how an AI actually learns,
                and at what it can and can't do once it has.
                That's what the rest of this chapter is about.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 2. Podcast                                                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <section xml:id="sec-demystifying-ai-podcast">
        <title>What Is AI Really Doing?</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                The ideas in this section come from a conversation between me
                and David Stern, from the IDEMS Podcast
                (<xref ref="biblio-podcast-072" text="custom">Clements and Stern, n.d.</xref>).
                It's about half an hour long, and it's part of a series on
                responsible AI for lecturers.
                You don't need to listen to it before reading on, but it's
                worth a listen at some point.
                It's also available at
                <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/072-demystifying-data-science/">idems.international</url>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-demystifying-ai-podcast">
                <caption>The IDEMS Podcast, episode 72: Demystifying Data Science</caption>
                <interactive xml:id="int-demystifying-ai-podcast"
                             iframe="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/4202/072-demystifying-data-science.mp3"
                             width="100%"
                             aspect="8:1"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The case study showed that people are involved in making AI
                safe.
                Now we want to look at why, by looking at how AI systems learn,
                and where that learning goes wrong.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-postcodes">
            <title>Learning from mistakes: the Post Office</title>

            <p>
                David starts with an old example, from before the recent
                advances in generative AI.
                For a long time, people sorted the post by hand.
                They read the address, and the postcode, on each envelope.
                Then, around the 1990s, machine learning made it possible to
                do some of that automatically.
            </p>

            <p>
                David's description is that the postcode on the envelope was
                turned into boxes with dots in them, and models were trained to
                recognise which characters those were.
                Once it could read the postcode, the machine could send the
                letter to the right place.
            </p>

            <p>
                At the start, it wasn't very good.
                It got things right about nine times in ten.
                And to begin with, good human sorters did better than the
                machines.
                But when the machine got a letter wrong, the mistake was
                caught somewhere else, and the letter was sent back to be read
                again.
                That correction was then fed back in, so the system learned
                from it.
                Over time, the machines got better than the people.
            </p>

            <p>
                David points out one reason.
                An experienced sorter gets better at the job, but eventually
                they leave, and their experience goes with them.
                It can't be passed on perfectly to the next person.
                When a machine learns something, it isn't lost.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    This is a good picture to keep in mind of what AI is doing.
                    It isn't working things out for itself.
                    It's learning from people who correct it, and the
                    corrections don't get lost.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                Look back at the case study with that in mind.
                The workers in Nairobi were doing the same sort of job as the
                people correcting the postcode readers, because they were the
                ones telling the system what it was looking at.
                David's point is that this is central to any AI system that's
                out there at the moment.
                The system thrives when there are humans in the loop, mistakes
                are fed back, and the corrections line up with what's true.
            </p>

            <p>
                There's a condition hidden in this, though.
                In the Post Office, mistakes get caught, because somebody
                notices when a letter ends up in the wrong place.
                The learning only works if there's a way for errors to be
                noticed and fed back.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-toad">
            <title>When nobody checks: the toad</title>

            <p>
                That brings us to David's other favourite story, which is
                about birdsong.
            </p>

            <p>
                There are apps that identify birds from their song, and they're
                brilliant.
                You can have one on your phone.
                What David struggles to see is how humans could be put in the
                loop so that these apps keep improving, until you could rely
                on them for something like conservation.
            </p>

            <p>
                He gives a concrete example.
                A colleague of ours used one of these apps, and it claimed
                there was a very rare bird nearby.
                When they tracked it down, the sound was coming from a toad,
                and the app had got it wrong.
            </p>

            <p>
                That might sound like a bit of fun.
                But imagine you were counting rare birds on a lake, to decide
                how much to spend on protecting them, and the app kept
                mistaking a population of toads for the rare bird.
                Your count would be wrong, and you might make the wrong
                decision about a species that really did need help.
                As David puts it, relying purely on the app could have really
                negative consequences.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <p>
                    An AI doesn't know the truth by default.
                    It only knows the truth that it's been given, and it can
                    bring out things that are simply wrong without anyone
                    noticing.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                So with any AI tool, a useful question is: how would anyone
                find out if it was wrong?
                For postcodes, the answer is easy.
                For birdsong, it's much harder, because very few people are
                going to go out and check each bird.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-style">
            <title>Sounding right isn't the same as being right</title>

            <p>
                I make the point in the podcast that this is what's difficult
                about the limits of AI.
                ChatGPT sounds so confident, and so do the birdsong apps.
                Nothing in the way they speak tells you when they're wrong.
            </p>

            <p>
                Generative AI does have some ways of collecting feedback.
                Sometimes it asks you to rate an answer, or it gives you two
                answers and asks which you prefer.
                That feeds back into its training.
                But, as David says, that helps with preference, not substance.
                If you pick the answer you like more, you're telling it about
                style.
                You aren't telling it which one is true.
            </p>

            <p>
                David explains that the advance that has blown people away is
                really about style.
                Generative AI can now write in a way that's very hard to tell
                from a person.
                It has, in effect, passed what's called the Turing test.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-demystifying-ai-turing">
                <title>The Turing test</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        In the <term>Turing test</term>, a person observes
                        conversations between humans, and conversations between
                        a human and an AI.
                        If the observer can't tell which are with the AI, the
                        AI has passed.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <p>
                David gives an example from education.
                He describes a case at the University of Reading where, out of
                33 assignments written with AI, only two were identified as
                AI.
                There are also stories of students whose own work was wrongly
                flagged as AI.
                Telling AI writing from human writing is hard, and David
                expects it to get harder.
                (This is David's account of the case, and we haven't checked
                it.)
            </p>

            <p>
                How does it manage this?
                David's explanation is that it learns from an enormous amount
                of text from the internet.
                On top of that data sit advanced mathematical models, which
                he says aren't yet well understood.
                Then there's a learning process, in which it works out which
                styles people like.
                Often that's a very human process, so you need humans in the
                loop to make the learning happen, which is something people
                forget.
            </p>

            <p>
                The result is that, for many subjects, AI can do a reasonable
                job, but it doesn't understand the substance, and it can't
                reliably give you the correct answer.
                David's view is that AI won't replace expert knowledge, or the
                ability to fact check, for quite a while yet.
                It struggles with nuance, because words that mean very
                different things can look very close together as data.
                And it's hard to train a system to tell the difference between
                something that's true and something that could have been true.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-moving-line">
            <title>A moving line</title>

            <p>
                So what can AI do, and what can't it?
                The honest answer is that nobody knows exactly, and the line
                keeps moving.
            </p>

            <p>
                David's example is Amazon's shops where you could pick up your
                shopping and walk out without going through a checkout.
                Amazon eventually pulled back from it and moved to smart
                shopping carts.
                David's account is that the AI models weren't yet good enough,
                so people in India, paid very little, watched video of shoppers
                to work out what had gone in each basket.
                In his words, that wasn't AI at all, it was human intelligence.
                Amazon has disputed how much human review was involved, and
                the next chapter looks at that story in more detail.
            </p>

            <p>
                David's point is that Amazon did have AI models.
                The problem was just too complicated for them yet.
                In five or ten years it might be possible.
                But nobody can say where the boundary is right now, because it
                keeps changing.
            </p>

            <p>
                That matters for anyone who's going to work alongside AI.
                The next generation will keep updating what's possible and
                what isn't, so they need to understand how these systems work
                well enough to judge that for themselves.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-demystifying-ai-summary">
            <title>Summary</title>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        AI systems get better by learning from human feedback,
                        and the corrections aren't lost.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        That only works if mistakes can be noticed and fed
                        back.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        An AI knows only what it's been given, so it can be
                        confidently wrong without anyone noticing.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Generative AI has got very good at style, which isn't
                        the same as substance.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The line between what AI can and can't do keeps moving,
                        and nobody knows exactly where it will settle.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>
    </section>

