    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <!-- 5. Exercises                                                   -->
    <!-- ============================================================= -->
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-demystifying-ai">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-1">
            <title>Who is responsible?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    OpenAI says that Sama was responsible for the workers' pay
                    and for their mental health support.
                    Sama says it offered counselling and followed the law.
                    The workers say they were badly paid and left to cope on
                    their own.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            Who do you think was responsible for looking after
                            the workers, and why?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Why does it get harder to say who's responsible when
                            the work is outsourced?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What could OpenAI have asked for in its contract, to
                            make sure the workers were looked after?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-2">
            <title>Whose figures do you believe?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    TIME, Sama and the workers disagree about how many passages
                    people had to read in a shift: 150 to 250, 70, and up to 700.
                    Suppose you wanted to find out which is right.
                    What evidence would you ask for, and who would have it?
                    What would you do if you couldn't get it?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-3">
            <title>Data labelling</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain data labelling to a friend who has never heard of
                    it, in a few sentences.
                    Then give an example of something you use that probably
                    depends on it, and say what you'd want to know about the
                    people who did the labelling.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-4">
            <title>Learning from mistakes</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The Post Office system got better because its mistakes were
                    caught and fed back.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            What had to be true for that to work?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Think of another system where mistakes would be
                            caught and fed back in the same way, and one where
                            they wouldn't.
                            Explain the difference.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Where in your own life do you rely on something
                            automatic, like a map, a translation tool or a spam
                            filter?
                            How would a mistake get noticed?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-5">
            <title>The toad</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Imagine you're counting a rare bird at a lake in Kenya, and
                    you're using an app that identifies birds by their song.
                    Think about these questions.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            How could you build a check into the way you use the
                            app?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            What would that check cost, in time and in money?
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            If you couldn't afford the check, what would you
                            say about the count when you reported it?
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-6">
            <title>Style and substance</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Pick a topic you know really well.
                    Ask an AI assistant a few questions about it, and write
                    down what it got right, what it got wrong, and how sure it
                    sounded each time.
                    Then ask yourself whether someone who didn't know the topic
                    could have told the difference.
                    Please don't put anyone's personal details into the
                    assistant.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-7">
            <title>The Turing test</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In your own words, what does it tell us if an AI passes the
                    Turing test, and what doesn't it tell us?
                    Why isn't passing the test the same as being right?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-demystifying-ai-8">
            <title>A moving line</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Pick something that people say AI can't do yet.
                    What would need to change for it to be possible?
                    How would you tell if it had become possible, or if it only
                    looked like it had?
                    Who might be doing some of the work behind the scenes?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

